 "caption": "Visibility of open-review work in the published paper, round 1. Papers: 58 of the 64 Copernicus papers in round 1 (2022); the open discussion was fetched for 61, and 3 review codings failed validation. 33 of the 58 are in The Cryosphere, so the result mostly describes one journal. The open discussion (referee, author, community and editor comments, posted beside a citable discussion preprint) was coded as a sequence of review steps by Claude Opus 5.5 (codebook 1.5.0, graph v0.4.2), which also read the published paper. Each of the 746 review steps was then labelled against the published text (publisher XML) by two model labelers, separate runs of a Claude model inside the Claude Code tool (model id not recorded), each without seeing the other's labels; a third run settled disagreements. The labelers agreed on 720 of 746 steps (96.5%; Cohen's kappa 0.91 over the four labels: agreement corrected for the agreement expected by chance from each labeler's label frequencies, 1 perfect, 0 chance) before adjudication. (a) Of 746 review steps, 580 record a change to the work; 560 of these appear in the paper as if planned, 20 do not appear, and 0 are marked as a revision. The interval on the absent share is a 95% percentile interval from 5000 bootstrap resamples of papers (1.7% to 5.6%), because changes cluster within papers. Every quote offered as evidence of a shown change exists verbatim in the published text (560 of 560); this checks that the quote exists, not that it shows the change. (b) Share of changes absent from the paper by graph node of the review step, nodes with at least 5 changes (left out: E (Explore, 2)); points are proportions, bars Wilson 95% intervals that treat steps as independent and are not adjusted for the 14 comparisons; counts at right; the dashed line is the pooled share. Interval excluding the pooled share: V (Vary or replicate, 13 of 102, absences in 10 papers). With papers as units (absent labels shuffled among the changes within each paper, 5000 permutations; Benjamini-Hochberg over the 14 nodes), V's absent share exceeds that of the other nodes by 11.3 percentage points (95% interval over papers 4.1 to 19.6, q = 0.003); other nodes with q < 0.1: none. Exploratory. (c) One column per paper, height = changes recorded in its review, split into absent and shown as planned; no paper marks any change as a revision (0 of 58; one-sided 95% upper bound by the rule of three, 3/58 = 5.2%). Changes per paper: median 10, range 4 to 18; 13 papers have at least one absent change, at most 4 in one paper. Columns are anonymous and unordered by journal."
